Browser locale and intake tags are intake guesses, not the grouping
rule. The table's ticket-ownership labels describe the original export,
not the new GitHub issues.

## Separate tickets

- [Shared parser: month-first reading for day-first tenants](https://github.com/taras42/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1).
  For example, R-41200 reports 7 April becoming 4 July.
- [booking-v3: displayed date hint conflicts with tenant interpretation](https://github.com/taras42/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2).
  For example, R-41241 reports a booking for 6 August appearing on 8 June.

The counts shown above describe the KB classifier's assignments.
They are not proof that every assigned report has the same root cause.

## Reports that need individual investigation

`desk-notes.json` flags R-41202, R-41209, R-41236, R-41344, and R-41620.
The classifier includes them in the parser group.

Their bodies describe one-day differences, an invite arriving before
booking, or different days for people in different locations. These
are not clear month/day swaps.

<Callout kind="warn">
  Keep these reports visible in the parser ticket and investigate them
  individually. Do not count them again as another confirmed cause.
  The export does not establish a timezone bug or another shared cause.
</Callout>

## The desk kept receiving and merging reports

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The original incident was reviewed on 2026-09-30. R-41620 was received
on 2026-10-04 and merged that evening. Marking the incident known did
not stop incoming reports or establish that they were resolved.

The timeline distinguishes when reports arrived from when they were
merged. A merge-date spike is not necessarily an arrival-date spike.

## Proposed triage correction

The [written triage rule](/kb/triage/dedupe) and
[accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)
merge by matching subject lines. The executable explorer instead
compares classified causes. These are different rules.

Use subject matches to find candidates, not to prove duplication:

1. Read the body and check tenant, locale, form version, and channel.
2. Merge only when the evidence supports the same failure mechanism.
3. Keep different causes separate even when their subjects match.
4. Investigate unknown or contradictory cases rather than merging by default.
5. Recompute report counts and review new arrivals before claiming resolution.

This is a proposed replacement policy, not an accepted decision.
Leave decision 0001 unchanged; record an accepted replacement in a
new numbered decision if the desk adopts this policy.

## Verification limits

The parser closure records engineering's reported fix. The hint closure
records retirement of the affected form, not a hint fix.

The export does not include production test results or individual
verification of the unusual reports. Preserve those qualifications
rather than treating ticket closure as proof about every report.

